cartridge "SURVIVOR/Kevin Mykytyn and Mark Tuttle/1986"

' SURVIVOR
' A ColecoVision game based on "Survivor", COMPUTE!'s Gazette March 1986
' (Commodore 64, also Plus/4: a BASIC loader and title, "survivor 1", and
' machine language at $2800, "survivor 2"; no author named in the files).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/survivor2.dis, and its BASIC, orig/survivor 1.bas,
' and watched and timed in jsc64), the game is rebuilt for the
' ColecoVision: drawn city blocks, smooth sprites, sound effects and a
' title tune.
'
' The game (as the C64 code plays it): two survivors duel in a city of
' 8 x 6 blocks (streets between and round them). Player 1 starts in the
' top-left corner, player 2 in the bottom-right. Each has 5 lives and 10
' shots a round. The stick walks along the streets (only while held); the
' button (pressed, not held) fires a shot the way you last walked. Four
' monsters leave the centre and wander the streets (at a crossing they go
' on, or turn left or right, never back). A monster, the hunter or a shot
' touching a survivor kills him; a shot that meets a monster sends it back
' to the centre. (Port: all of a frame's hits are checked first, then
' applied: two survivors hit in the same frame both lose a life, and if
' that leaves both with none the game is a draw, NOBODY SURVIVED. The C64
' checked player 2 first, the first draft of this port blue first.) When a survivor dies everything stops for the burst, he
' loses a life, and a new round starts (both back to their corners, the
' monsters to the centre, 10 shots each, bonuses gone). The last one with
' lives is the SURVIVOR. Bonus signs appear now and then on empty streets
' (each of the four kinds by chance, about one in 128 every monster step)
' and fade after a while:
'   SWAP (arrows)    the two survivors change places; the sign goes
'   SPEED (bolt)     walk twice as fast for about 27 s; the sign stays
'   SHOTS (crate)    stand on it: one more shot every 1.3 s (up to 10)
'   HUNTER (skull)   a hunter comes out of the top-left corner after the
'                    OTHER survivor for about 95 s; shots do not stop it
' Speeds (measured in jsc64 against the 60 Hz clock, then per pixel: a
' C64 street cell is 6 of our pixels): survivors 0.31 px a frame (0.62
' with SPEED), monsters 0.25, the hunter 0.19, shots 1.
'
' Additions: a ONE PLAYER mode against the computer (the C64 game is for
' two players only; the computer plays the green survivor through the
' same stick rules, looking at the street every 16 frames).
'
' Liberties: the city keeps the C64's 8 x 6 blocks, drawn 2 x 2 tiles
' with one-tile streets (the C64: 3 x 3 characters); actors glide pixel by
' pixel instead of jumping a character at a time; contact is a 6-pixel
' box; holding a direction the street does not allow keeps you walking to
' the next corner, where you turn (the C64 just stopped you); a shot
' towards a wall is not fired and costs nothing (the C64 lost it); the
' SHOTS crate gives its shots at a fixed beat (the C64 counted down a
' stray byte); a burst lasts 2.5 s (the C64's three slow frames, about
' 4 s); the countdown 3-2-1 shows in the centre of the city (the C64: in
' its side panel); the winner is named, then the title (the C64 asked to
' press the button to play again).
'
' Controls: controller 1 walks the blue survivor, controller 2 the green
' one (in ONE PLAYER mode either controller walks blue). The stick walks,
' either button fires. On the title, up and down choose a line, a button
' starts (or shows the credits).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

